# CLAUDE.md — {{PROJECT_NAME}}

Django 6 (ASGI) + Next.js App Router monorepo, cloned from `base-scaffold {{SCAFFOLD_VERSION}}`.
Functionality arrives as **versioned, read-only app packages**; this repo is the host that
wires them together.

**Read `docs/INTEGRATION-GUIDE.md` in full before any task that installs an app, touches
`core/`, or crosses two apps.** This file is the fast reference for everything else.

## The one rule that matters most

Anything under `backend/.venv`/`site-packages` or `frontend/node_modules` is **READ-ONLY**.
Never edit an installed app package — not to fix a bug, not to tweak a default, not once.
Escalation path: config issue → `backend/config/`; cross-app backend → `backend/core/`;
cross-app UI → `frontend/app/`; different view behavior → subclass in `core/views/`;
real bug in the app → a change in **that app's own repo**, released and re-pinned.
Full table: `docs/INTEGRATION-GUIDE.md` §1.

`backend/uv.lock` is machine-generated. Never hand-edit it, and never change a dependency
line without `uv add` / `uv lock`.

## Commands

Prefer `make`; it's the single place these are defined.

| Command | What it does |
|---|---|
| `make up` | Start the dev stack (`docker compose up --build`) |
| `make down` / `make logs` | Stop / tail |
| `make test` | Host test suite against an ephemeral Postgres |
| `make lint` / `make fmt` / `make typecheck` | ruff + eslint / format / mypy + tsc |
| **`make check`** | **lint + typecheck + test — the definition of done** |
| `make migrate` / `make migrations` | Apply / create migrations |
| `make shell` | `shell_plus` in the backend container |
| `make deploy` | `./deploy/deploy-prod.sh` |

Installing an app package (full protocol: `docs/INTEGRATION-GUIDE.md` §2):

```bash
cd backend  && uv add "git+https://github.com/{{ORG}}/<app>.git@vX.Y.Z#subdirectory=backend"
cd frontend && npm install "github:{{ORG}}/<app>#vX.Y.Z:frontend"
# then: copy the app's README config block into config/settings.py, add .env keys,
# mount URLs, migrate, add its banned-api line, `docker compose up --build`,
# and update the table below.
```

**Both halves must be at the same tag.** A restart without `--build` will not pick up a
new package — that's the most common "I installed it but it isn't there."

